# {{project_name}} — Execution protocol ({{initiative_title}})
> **Protocol:** adcm-toolkits 0.22.2
> **Reviewer:** {{reviewer_handle}} ({{forge}}) · **Merge policy:** wait

> **What this is.** The operating manual of the build. It defines HOW the catalog in
> `detailed-plan.md` gets executed: **phase sessions** (one clean session per phase of a wave,
> a handoff file between them — not 1 task = 1 session),
> self-verified loops with the **DoD-auto as the exit criterion**, workflow fan-out
> where parallelism helps, **one code-simplifier pass over every wave's integrated
> diff before the final DoD run**, and **mandatory adversarial verification at
> ⚠gates**. The
> copy-paste prompts per phase are at the end (§7). Keep in sync with `task.md`
> (state) and `master-plan.md` (rationale). Prompts are tuned for Fable at high
> effort but run on any model.

## 1. Principles

1. **One clean session per phase; one branch/MR per wave of 1–4 coupled tasks.** A wave runs as
   phases a implement + DoD · b gate · c gate fixes · d doc-sync + courier close · e production close
   (§4). Each phase ends with its handoff file and the `HANDOFF: <path>` line and never continues into
   the next phase in the same session; hooks guard the main thread of every phase session (§4).
2. **The loop ends by DoD, not by fatigue.** Each wave defines its **DoD-auto**: a
   list 100% verifiable by command. While it isn't green the loop continues:
   implement → verify → fix. Max 5 rounds; if the SAME failure doesn't converge in
   **3 attempts** → stop, report, **never degrade tests to pass**. Then ONE simplify
   pass (§1.11) → re-verify: the FULL DoD-auto runs AGAIN after it, so the green DoD
   is always the DoD of the shipped diff.
3. **DoD-auto ≠ DoD-human.** Actions only {{owner}} can take (dashboards, DNS,
   purchases, approvals) live in the **DoD-human**: when the DoD-auto is exhausted
   the task is marked `blocked` with the human checklist rendered at close. It only
   becomes `completed` when {{owner}} confirms.
4. **Implementers never self-approve at gates.** On ⚠gate waves the executor is an
   `adcm-toolkits:executor` called with `model: opus` and the wave does NOT reach phase d without **adversarial
   verification** (phase b, its own clean session) by ANOTHER agent, an `adcm-toolkits:auditor` (the reserved one — never the
   executor itself, never the orchestrator's tier) that did NOT see the
   implementation, given only the diff + the DoD + that wave's attack checklist (§6). Its
   brief carries `REPORT PATH: {{docs_dir}}/qa/gate-<wave>.md` (wave id in lowercase) — or the brain's existing layout (e.g. `qa/<wave>/gate-*.md` when `qa/` already has per-wave folders; never change an existing brain's convention): it returns the full report under
   `REPORT:` and the orchestrator saves it verbatim (a mechanical save, not an edit).
   This covers the simplifier too: it edits code, so it is an implementer — the
   orchestrator re-runs the DoD over its edits, and on ⚠gate waves the simplifier is
   neither the executor nor the reserved verifier, and runs BEFORE the verification.
5. **Inviolable decisions** (no session may contradict them — from
   `master-plan.md` §8): {{list_them_inline}}.
6. **No hardcoded dates**: the logbook uses the date of the day of execution.
7. **Agent budget 20 with fixed roles.** The main session (Fable) ORCHESTRATES:
   it decides design and scope, audits the deliverables `opus` investigators return
   (solution · files · considerations · open questions), writes one executor brief
   per task, runs every DoD-slice and the full DoD-auto itself, and integrates —
   it never implements first and never spawns its own tier. `opus` agents
   investigate (`adcm-toolkits:researcher`, called with `model: opus`), review regression
   and verify (`adcm-toolkits:auditor`) (quota 10 per phase session; reserved — the
   simplify pass in phase a, the ⚠gate verifier in phase b; audits pass to `sonnet` once
   the rest is spent), and the ⚠gate executor is `adcm-toolkits:executor` + `model: opus`.
   `sonnet` agents (`adcm-toolkits:executor`; `adcm-toolkits:executor-frontend` on UI and frontend-code waves — when the wave loads design skills OR the task's FILES are frontend app code (components, pages, hooks/composables, client services/stores); cost: the `frontend-design` preload (~3.1k tokens)) implement NO-gate tasks from briefs, ≤4 in
   parallel with disjoint files in their own worktrees (a shared helper is not a
   disjoint file: the task that extracts it closes before its consumers start), and
   never self-approve. ONE packaged `code-simplifier:code-simplifier` pass per wave,
   called with an explicit `model: opus` (one tier down with the roles when the main
   session is not Fable), costs 1 of the 20 and 1 of the Opus quota and is verified by
   the orchestrator, never by itself. One delivery `adcm-toolkits:courier` per batch (normally 1; each
   extra batch = 1 more of the 20, 0 Opus quota) — `sonnet` ALWAYS, even when the main
   session is not Fable — costs 1 of the 20 (the first is reserved, like the gate
   verifier) and 0 Opus quota (§2b step 7).
   **Escalation ladder** Sonnet → Opus → orchestrator: the 2nd failure of the SAME
   DoD line moves the task one tier up as a new agent carrying the attempt log.
   One counter per phase session, `model` explicit on every call or an `adcm-toolkits:*` type
   (a per-call `model` overrides the type's default: `researcher` + `model: opus` for Opus investigations, `executor` + `model: opus` for ⚠gate waves and Opus-written sections, `auditor` + `model: sonnet` once the Opus quota is spent or when the main session is one tier down; types fix the TOOL SET, the tier rules still set the model), `SendMessage` to a live agent costs 0. Ultracode changes the orchestration (Workflow per stage + adversarial
   cross-check), never roles or count. Exceeding 20 is a protocol violation; the
   logbook records `agents used: n/20 (opus a · sonnet b)` with each agent's role
   (investigate / execute / review / simplify / verify / capture / courier) and every escalation.
   Other packaged agents still name their `model`: the plugin's frontmatter is
   irrelevant. The role agents load at the next session or `/reload-plugins`; no plugin in
   this session → `Agent(subagent_type: "general-purpose", model: "<tier>")` with the same
   brief. On the orchestrator's own last-rung fixes (≤~20 lines,
   when a brief would cost more than the change): every one is LOGGED in the
   `task.md` logbook (`orchestrator fix: <file> — <why a brief cost more>`); an
   unlogged orchestrator edit is a protocol violation, not a shortcut. Saving a sub-agent's
   RETURN verbatim to the path its brief names (gate reports `qa/gate-<wave>.md`, wave id in lowercase, or the brain's existing `qa/<wave>/gate-*.md` layout, digests) is a
   mechanical save, not an edit. The main-thread guard hooks enforce what follows (§4). The orchestrator
   is otherwise PURE: it never reads files in bulk, never edits at scale, never renders,
   browses or publishes, and every `Agent`/`Workflow` call names its `model` (Plan and
   Explore agents included) or is an `adcm-toolkits:*` type. The user's phrase `sin tanto lío` (that one task) or
   `modo directo` (until `modo orquestador`) switches this off and the main session
   does the work itself — rule and tier table in the skill's
   `references/orchestrator-rule.md`.
8. **Executor brief.** What the orchestrator hands to an executor, ≤40 lines:
   `TASK · FILES (from the Scope manifest — the shared helper and EVERY consumer to
   rewire included when the manifest says so — plus the `Map:` entries, read only those entries (plus the ONE `rg` lookup the agent's Reuse rule allows before writing an unnamed helper)) · SKILLS (the wave's Skills to load —
   the executor loads them with `Skill` before starting) · DELIVERABLE (Opus's audited deliverable
   with the orchestrator's decisions) · DESIGN DECISIONS (incl. the reuse verdict —
   `consume` · `extract` · `deliberate duplicate` · `none`, *family* as a qualifier — and
   the helper's home — not negotiable) · DOD-SLICE (command →
   expected; an extraction adds every rewired consumer's build/test and the duplication
   census → 1) · STOP IF (stop and report instead of improvising — including a consumer
   the census does not name or a helper home crossing repos; it fires on duplication
   only when the deliverable would have to WRITE a duplicate (logic or shape) the brief
   does not name — a sighting goes to the `RULE-OF-TWO` line and the executor continues) ·
   FORBIDDEN (paths outside the brief — the named helper and its
   consumers ARE in scope —, dependency changes, no hand-written variant of a type a TS
   utility type derives, no edit to `{{docs_dir}}/map/`, and comments narrating decisions,
   rationale or plan references, §1.10) ·
   RETURN (diff summary per file · commands + output · what was extracted or consumed
   and which consumers were rewired, as the agent's `REUSED` line · its `RULE-OF-TWO`
   line · open questions)`. Every §7
   prompt carries one pre-filled block per task, plus the wave's `## Simplifier brief`
   (§1.11). A poor brief produces poor code.

9. **REUSE before writing — decided in planning.** Logic that already exists is
   consumed; logic two or more components use or need is extracted ONCE into a shared
   helper both consume (rule of TWO, guarded by the same-behavior test: extract only
   when both consumers would change together — otherwise it is a deliberate duplicate
   with its why recorded). Pattern family — the rule of TWO applied to SHAPE: two or more entities/components with the same shape (CRUD/API calls per entity, list/detail/form, table columns, validation, data fetching) share ONE generic base (an abstract class with generics, or a generic function/hook/composable) and each member supplies only its model/type and endpoint (`ProvidersService extends BaseApiService<Provider>`). TS utility types (`Partial`, `Pick`, `Omit`, `Record`, `ReturnType`, `Parameters`, `Awaited`, `NonNullable`) replace hand-written variants. Least code: the minimum lines, zero duplicates. The same-behavior guard holds per member; a base forced onto members that would not change together is over-abstraction. The verdict is exactly one of `consume` ·
   `extract` · `deliberate duplicate` · `none`; *family* qualifies it, never a fifth verdict
   (`consume family: <base path> → member <file> (supplies <Model> + <endpoint>)` ·
   `extract family: <base path> (<Base><T>) <- members: <Entity: file, …> (each supplies only
   its model/type + endpoint)` — card form; SCOPE lines use the compact form of §2). The map is consulted in PLANNING (verdicts and `Map:` lines
   cite its entries) and updated only by its script at close (§2b step 6), never by hand.
   The verdict travels in the task's Scope manifest and in the
   prompt's `# SCOPE`; an executing session APPLIES it and runs the duplication census
   FIRST, it never hunts for duplication. The helper plus the rewiring of ALL its
   current consumers is ONE task — every consuming task lists it under `Depends on`, so
   it closes before they start and is never parallelised with them — and its DoD covers
   every rewired consumer. Duplication found anyway is reported (the executor's `RULE-OF-TWO` line), not improvised on: if
   every implicated file is already in this wave's SCOPE the orchestrator amends the
   briefs and extracts in-wave (helper first, consumers after, serialized); if any file
   is outside, it goes to the logbook `Reuse:` field as a follow-up candidate and into
   the later-wave manifests at doc-sync (§2b step 3).
10. **COMMENTS explain the WHY, never the plan.** Allowed: the why of non-obvious
    logic (an invariant, a workaround for a named external bug, a gotcha, a
    non-obvious algorithm), public-API docstrings in this repo's existing convention,
    TODOs pointing at the repo's issue tracker when it has one. Not allowed: restating
    what the code does, decision or rationale narration ("we chose X because"), change
    narration ("added in wave 2", "moved from"), plan references (task IDs, wave
    numbers, execution-doc names) and commented-out code. The reason: decisions already
    live in `master-plan.md` (§1 with its rejected alternatives, §3, §4, §8), in the
    `detailed-plan.md` cards and in the `task.md` logbook — a comment repeating them is
    a second record that rots. One Base DoD line makes the plan-reference half
    command-verifiable; narration vs why is a judgement call and belongs to the `opus`
    regression review and to the simplifier, never to a fuzzy grep.
11. **SIMPLIFY — one pass per wave.** Once the regression findings are fixed, the
    orchestrator makes a checkpoint commit and runs the packaged agent
    `code-simplifier:code-simplifier` with an explicit `model: opus` over the wave's
    integrated diff, driven by the `## Simplifier brief` (≤20 lines: the diff's file
    list and nothing else · the project conventions override the agent's built-in style
    defaults · the executor briefs' design decisions are not negotiable · the comment
    policy of §1.10 · no behavior, dependency, formatting-only or test changes ·
    returns a diff summary per file and what it skipped). It costs 1 of the 20 and 1 of
    the Opus quota, never self-approves, and on ⚠gate waves runs BEFORE the reserved
    verifier. Cap: ONE pass, edits applied in ONE round, never a simplify → verify
    loop. If the FULL DoD-auto fails after it: ONE fix round to the SAME simplifier
    (`SendMessage`, cost 0 — if it cannot be reached, skip straight to the revert); a
    second failure goes back to the checkpoint commit (`git reset --hard` while the
    branch is unpushed, `git revert` of the simplify commit if already pushed) and the
    wave closes on the pre-simplify diff, recorded in the logbook. That retry consumes
    neither the 5 rounds nor the 3 attempts. The simplifier edits the wave branch in
    place; the orchestrator commits its diff. The brief is the contract and the packaged
    agent only its default carrier: where `code-simplifier:code-simplifier` is not
    installed, the same `## Simplifier brief` goes to ONE `adcm-toolkits:executor` with
    `model: <auditor tier>` (the auditor type has no Edit) — same model, cost, cap and RETURN — and the logbook `Simplifier:` field records which
    carrier ran.
12. **Claude never merges.** A wave ends with its MR open, pipeline green and simplify
    pass done, assigned to the Reviewer (`> **Reviewer:**` above); the merge is a
    DoD-human item and wave N+1 starts only after MR N is merged (§3).

## 2. Canonical phase prompts

One self-contained prompt per phase (§4, §7); `wave-prompt.tmpl` holds the full template. Every prompt opens with the
§7 first line and the 3-line MAIN-THREAD GUARD note and ends with the PHASE CLOSE lines (§4).

**Phase a — implement + DoD**

```text
Start a clean session in EXECUTION mode — <project> · Wave <Wx> · PHASE a (implement + DoD)
MAIN-THREAD GUARD — hooks cap this session at 25 Bash and 8 Read calls, deny heavy reads (whole docs, long ranges, images)
and edits over 20 lines, force the handoff at the 2nd compaction and lock the session at the 3rd. Delegate from the FIRST
call: you brief, launch, read RETURNs, run the DoD and decide; sub-agents read, edit, render, judge and publish.
Auto-accept edits, never plan mode: the plan IS this prompt (# SCOPE); the only re-planning trigger is a diverging census.
0. `plan-audit --brain {{docs_dir}} --check --wave <Wx>` (the `plan-audit --check` seal gate) must exit 0; otherwise ⛔ stop
   before any code (handoff `Result: fail`).
1. Load the skill `{{project_context_skill}}`; never read execute.md or task.md whole. 2. PRECONDITION: the previous wave's MR
   is merged (omit on the first wave). 3. `status-digest --brain {{docs_dir}}` (bare; without the plugin: `python3
   {{docs_dir}}/scripts/status_digest.py --brain {{docs_dir}}`): deps ✅, no foreign `[PARTIAL]` or open phase. Exit 2 → one
   `adcm-toolkits:digester` with `{{docs_dir}}/scripts/status-brief.md`; without an Agent tool, stop and report.

# GOAL — Wave <Wx>: <titles>
Work EXACTLY the tasks <IDs> (specs: detailed-plan.md — read them in full; they are
the specification).
DoD-auto (the loop's EXIT criterion; everything by command — the FULL run happens
AFTER the simplify pass):
- [ ] <command> → <expected result>
- [ ] <the comment-hygiene command from detailed-plan §0 Base DoD, with this wave's base branch> → 0
- [ ] (if a task extracts a helper) <build/test of EVERY rewired consumer> → exit 0 · <dup census> → 1
- [ ] (if the wave touches UI) a sub-agent REVIEWED the render — see "VISUAL CHECK"

# TASKS
Create one task per ID (TaskCreate), mark it in_progress on entry and completed on
verification. Checkpoint commit (Conventional) at each internal task close.

# SCOPE (manifest)
<per task, inlined from its Scope manifest card in detailed-plan.md: Modify/Create
with a 1-line why per file · Read first exemplars · Impact (shared surface ← N
consumers; contract kept or adaptation in scope; DoD covers the consumers) · Reuse in
1–2 lines (consume <path> | consume family <base> → member <file> | extract → <path>;
rewire <A,B,C> | extract family → <base><T>; members <A,B,C> (model + endpoint only) |
deliberate duplicate — <why> | none; the dup census rides the Census line) · Map: the
entries the task consumes, or none · Census
command → expected N · tolerance (exact/±1 for small counts; ±X% only for large) ·
Symbol notes when they fit the budget, else the pointer to the card. The
investigation already ran: do NOT re-derive these lists and do NOT hunt for
duplication — apply the reuse verdict. Census FIRST; divergence
beyond tolerance ⇒ STOP and re-scope against detailed-plan. Budget UNCHANGED ~15
lines/task (hard cap 25 for multi-file tasks), ≤60/wave — the reuse and Map lines live
inside the ~15, overflow stays in the card.>

# LOOP
brief → execute (sub-agent) → verify (YOU run the DoD-slice) → fix → repeat, then ONE
simplify pass → re-verify. Max 5
rounds; the executor's 2nd failure of the same DoD line escalates the task one tier
up (sonnet → opus → you) with the attempt log; 3 attempts on the same failure at
session level → stop and report (don't degrade tests, don't mark done what isn't
done). The simplify pass consumes no round: a DoD failure after it gets ONE fix round
to the same simplifier, then a revert to the checkpoint commit.

# VISUAL CHECK (MANDATORY if the wave touches UI: .pug/.html/.scss/.css/components)
grep + build only prove the code is THERE, not that it LOOKS right (they never catch real width,
wrong-language text, overflow/overlap). Before marking a UI task completed: delegate the capture to an
`adcm-toolkits:researcher` (`sonnet`; browser, no edits; 1 of the 20, 0 Opus quota; the main session does not render or screenshot; when this
brief's DoD includes the VISUAL CHECK, `executor-frontend` captures it itself — never both): it renders the page in headless Chrome — local
server (full env) or the deployed URL — with a screenshot helper (Playwright/Puppeteer + the system browser),
captures desktop (≥1280) AND mobile (375), both languages if i18n, and RETURNS the PNG paths plus its findings
(width/responsive (not "compacted"), i18n, no overlaps, design-system fonts/colors). The main session does NOT open
the PNGs: visual judgment is a sub-agent's job (capture researcher, executor-frontend VISUAL CHECK, auditor). Sub-agents
return `MEDIA: unsent: <paths>`; the main session sends them with SendUserFile by path WITHOUT opening them (load it with
ToolSearch `select:SendUserFile`); the guard denies image reads on the main thread.

