<MAIN-THREAD GUARD note, the same 3 lines as phase a>
status-digest → read ONLY the newest handoff (phase d, `Next phase: e`). PRECONDITION: the human merged the MR (and it is
deployed, when the wave ships), else stop. Deploying wave: doc-sync phase A (target `✅ in prod <date>`, markers SMOKE ·
POSTPROD · MAINTHREAD) in parallel with a `sonnet` smoke agent{{ and, on ⚠gate waves, an `opus` post-prod auditor
(`qa/gate-<wave>-postprod.md`, wave id in lowercase, saved verbatim)}} → handoff (`Next phase: none`) → doc-sync phase B
with the marker values → courier → media → PushNotification → final message with the PHASE CLOSE lines and LAST the links
block. Merge close (no deploy): verify the merge → handoff → the doc-sync executor flips the wave ✅ (A and B in ONE
message: MAINTHREAD is its only marker) → courier only if a registered page changed → close with `Next phase: none`.
```

## 2b. Doc-sync at close (mandatory — "append the logbook" means ALL of this)

Phase d closes a wave (gate PASS, or the DoD-auto green on a NO-gate plain wave) and phase e closes it in
production; each updates ALL plan docs in ONE pass, one Conventional commit. **The doc-sync is always ONE `sonnet`
executor in two phases**, briefed from `{{docs_dir}}/scripts/docsync-brief.md`; the main session never writes or edits
a sheet or a doc:
- **A** — the executor builds the sheet + markers file in the scratch folder the orchestrator names (steps 1–4, 7 and 8
  below as `docsync-apply` ops: sub · tick · insert_before · insert_after · append_rows · logbook), leaves
  `docsync-apply --check` clean (`docsync-apply --sheet <scratch>/sheet.json --markers <scratch>/markers.json --root {{docs_dir}} --check`:
  `DOCSYNC: <n> ok · 0 failed · <m> open placeholders`, open = only the markers phase B fills), writes no doc, and
  RETURNs that summary line, its doubts and the markers it still needs.
- **B** — by SendMessage to the SAME agent carrying the marker values (e.g. `MAINTHREAD`, `SKILLSYNC`, `SMOKE`,
  `POSTPROD`) and the answers to its doubts: `git pull --ff-only`, map-refresh (step 6, its `MAP:` line fills `MAP`),
  the same `--check` (0 failed, 0 open), `--write`, `status-digest` → `DIGEST: ok`, `git add` by explicit path, commit,
  push (step 9); after `--write` it also runs `plan-audit --brain {{docs_dir}} --check` and RETURNs its result line with
  the commit hash and the digest line. It never seals. Merge close with no marker to wait for: A and B go in ONE message.
- **Plan-audit after the doc-sync** (the sheet changes sealed text). Waves that are done or already started are exempt, so
  a wave's own phases are never withheld; whoever changes a not-started wave's plan re-audits it before closing. Exit 0 →
  continue; exit 2 → the doc-sync changed later waves: ONE `adcm-toolkits:auditor` runs `{{docs_dir}}/scripts/plan-audit-brief.md`
  in scoped mode on the stale waves now, its RETURN saved verbatim to `{{docs_dir}}/qa/plan-audit.md`, {{owner}} answers every
  contradiction, an executor applies the decisions, then `plan-audit --brain {{docs_dir}} --seal`. The phase does not close
  until the check exits 0. If the phase cannot finish after its handoff was written (owner away, session ends), rewrite
  that handoff as `Result: partial` with `Next phase:` the same phase (d or e) before stopping.
- In phase d the handoff is written BEFORE doc-sync B, so its stamped `**Main thread:**` line can be copied.
What the sheet covers:

1. **`task.md`** — logbook entry with the extended schema (date of execution ·
   branch/MR · tasks closed · **blocked** · **files touched** · **manifest drift** · notes · wall
   clock · **reuse** (helpers or pattern family bases consumed/extracted · consumers or members rewired · dup census
   before → after · unplanned duplication found (logic or shape) → follow-up | none) · **map** (map-refresh
   summary | not refreshed (why) | n/a) · **simplifier**
   (carrier: packaged | fallback sub-agent · ran over `{{checkpoint_sha}}..HEAD` · files
   touched · DoD re-verified | reverted (why) | not run (why)) · **agents used n/20 (opus a · sonnet b)** with roles investigate /
   execute / review / simplify / verify / capture / courier (record the NUMBER of couriers: 1, or more
   when the delivery ran in several batches), one line per phase session · then, right after it,
   `**Main thread:** <seg>[; <seg>…]` with seg = `[<phase> ]<n> Bash · <n> Read · <n> compactions`, copied from the
   stamped lines of the wave's handoffs (the digest flags ⚠ when a seg has compactions ≥ 2 or Bash > 25) ·
   **escalations** · **Next:** exact next phase prompt, or "wave closed") AND the wave-map status flip.
2. **`execute.md` §7** — this wave's phase headers flip (`☐` → `✅ DONE (date)` /
   `🔄` / `⛔`).
3. **`detailed-plan.md`** — mark the tasks done; update their Scope manifests with
   what was ACTUALLY touched; for every LATER wave whose manifest names surfaces this
   wave changed, refresh its file lists and census expected counts. A helper or
   pattern-family base born in this wave is written into the `Reuse:` verdict, the `Map:` line and the
   Read-first list of every later wave that needs the same logic, so the next wave consumes it instead of
   duplicating it again.
4. **`execute.md` §7** — regenerate the phase prompts of any later wave whose manifest
   changed in (3): its phase a `# SCOPE` must match detailed-plan.
5. **Delivery message — ENDS with the links block of the last courier RETURN, no filler.** The links
   come from the `artifact-courier` (step 7), never from the main session: its returned
   block holds (a) the wave's viewable result when it renders, ALWAYS AS TWO LINES —
   the `localhost` URL FIRST ({{owner}} validates from iTerm on the Mac) and the
   LAN-IP URL after it (so it also opens from a phone); never one without the other,
   and never the LAN IP alone: it changes with the network, so a link handed over
   yesterday arrives dead while `localhost` keeps working — and (b) ALWAYS the
   project's canonical artifacts from `{{docs_dir}}/artifacts.json` (plans, prompts,
   mockups…), even when this wave rendered nothing new. The orchestrator only starts
   the preview server if none runs (bound to `0.0.0.0`, with HTTP Range support or video
   is dead on iOS; same for a served PDF or report) and passes the localhost URL in the
   courier's brief; the courier reads the LAN IP (`ipconfig getifaddr en0`) and `curl`s
   both URLs before returning them.
   **Off the LAN — tailnet preview**: when the repo has a `tailnet-preview` profile (`tailnet-preview list`), the
   orchestrator starts the preview with `tailnet-preview up <slug> --apply` instead — detached (it outlives the session
   and keeps the Mac awake until `tailnet-preview down <slug> --apply` or the next wave's `up`) and mapped by
   `tailscale serve` to a tailnet-only HTTPS URL (never `funnel`). It passes that URL in the brief's `Tailnet:` field;
   the courier `curl`s it and returns a THIRD line — **the one exception to the two-line rule above** — right after the
   LAN one, above the artifacts: `- [🌐 <preview> · tailnet](https://…ts.net:<port>/…)`, which {{owner}} opens from the
   phone on any network. The orchestrator must dispatch on the verdict word, never on the exit code: `up` or `mapped` →
   `Tailnet: <url>`; `lan-only` (no Tailscale) → `Tailnet: none`, two lines as before; any other word → `Preview: none`
   and `Tailnet: none`, and the narrative and the 'Para ti' lines say why. With a tailnet line the 'Para ti' lines carry
   `Probado desde el cel → 🌐 <preview> · tailnet (abajo)` — the label, never the URL.
   **Order of the final message**: repo paths and commit hashes (one line each —
   reference, not delivery) and the pending DoD-human lines go ABOVE the links block,
   never after it: the guard rejects any text after the last link. The block itself is
   the last courier RETURN's `=== LINKS ===` tail pasted verbatim as the LAST lines — no headings,
   no text inside it.
   **Format — links must be TAPPABLE on a phone**: each link is a plain Markdown
   bullet `- [emoji Title](url)` — NEVER inside a code fence, inline backticks, or
   4-space indentation ({{owner}} reads closes on a phone, where code-formatted
   links render as dead text). The raw URL living inside the Markdown link also
   keeps Claude Code's quick-access footer badges (below the mode indicator)
   working — both surfaces, one format.
   **Media before the final message**: the courier returns the screenshots of the VISUAL
   CHECK (a GIF when the feature spans several screens) as `MEDIA: unsent: <paths>` and
   you send them with SendUserFile by path, without opening them (load SendUserFile with ToolSearch
   `select:SendUserFile` first; sub-agents cannot load it, so they never send); visual judgment is a
   sub-agent's job and the guard denies image reads on the main thread. The
   narrative comes first, then the files, then one `PushNotification` (`Claude finished in <folder>`, or
   `Claude needs you in <folder>` when the stop needs the owner or a gate failed; with `status: "proactive"`; deferred: `ToolSearch` `select:PushNotification`;
   absent → skip; "not sent" is fine, never retry; never from a sub-agent), then a SHORT final message — the pending
   DoD-human lines, the PHASE CLOSE lines (§4) and then the block — so on a phone {{owner}} sees the media and
   links without scrolling back up; nothing follows the block.
   The `artifact-guard` Stop hook blocks the close when a courier RETURN reached the turn and
   its block is missing from the final message.
6. **Project context skill — DELTA refresh** (a sibling `sonnet` executor in phase d; its one-line result is the
   `SKILLSYNC` marker): with the handoffs' files-touched list,
   update only the affected references of `{{project_context_skill}}` (usage-map
   consumer counts, folder inventories, entry hints if new surfaces were born) and
   bump their `last_scanned`. Use the generator's incremental mode
   (`scan_project.py --update <paths>`) when available. Every closed wave leaves the
   brain AND the code map fresh — freshness never depends on manual re-scans.
   Then the doc-sync executor (phase B) refreshes the brain's map with ONE call per repo the wave touched — `map-refresh --project <repo root>
   --brain {{docs_dir}} --update <files touched there> --human '<Symbol>@<path>=<what>|<when>'…` (bare
   launcher; one `--human` per `born` entry of the executors' `REUSED` lines; `--human` fills the ✍ cells of
   utilities rows; a pattern row's ✍ cells are edited in place, ✍ cells only) — never by re-deriving rows
   in prose: it adds or updates the auto rows of `map/utilities.md` and `map/patterns.md` for the helpers
   and pattern-family bases the wave created, keeps every human field, and its last line (`MAP: …`) goes
   into the logbook `Map:` field. No `map/` yet, or no plugin in this session → `Map: not refreshed
   (<why>)` (`renovate-check`'s MAP block brings it). The `map/` diff rides the step-9 commit+push of `{{docs_dir}}`.
7. **`master-plan.md` execution-state block, `plans.html` and `prompts.html` —
   REGENERATE AND REPUBLISH, by the courier**: flip the row/badge in master-plan's
   execution-state block and finish the doc-sync of steps 1–4, 6 (the map) and 9 — commit and push
   it FIRST (a project pre-commit may regenerate HTML when it commits the `.md`). The
   main session never opens, renders or edits brain HTML and never calls the Artifact
   tool: it delegates the delivery to one `adcm-toolkits:courier` per batch (it preloads the skill
   `adcm-toolkits:artifact-courier`; brief from its `templates/courier-brief.md`,
   `sonnet` fixed; normally 1 courier, each extra batch = 1 more of the 20, 0 Opus quota). The courier runs each registry
   row's `regen` (`plans-regen.py` for `plans.html`, `prompts-regen.py` for
   `prompts.html`, both copied with their `.tmpl` to `{{docs_dir}}/scripts/`), republishes
   every stale artifact to its SAME URL — or re-issues it as a new one above ~600 KB —
   stamps `published_at`/`version`/`sha256` in `{{docs_dir}}/artifacts.json`, commits
   that file, returns the media paths (`MEDIA: unsent`) and a table plus the links block. `prompts.html`
   changes whenever a §7 header flips or a prompt is regenerated, so BOTH pages are
   republished at practically every close; its only source of truth is the
   §7 phase headers (`### <Wave> · <phase> — <title>`; legacy `### Wave <ID>`). A badge flipped on disk without a republish leaves
   the shared link stale (lesson learned: a wave closed with the published plan still
   showing "pending"). New artifact ⇒ new row in `artifacts.json` (without `url`) in
   the same session: the courier publishes it. If the courier's RETURN carries
   `ERRORS`, send ONE `SendMessage` to the SAME courier (cost 0) and report — its new
   RETURN supersedes the earlier block; never publish yourself. The `artifact-guard` Stop hook blocks the close while a registered
   artifact is stale — it trusts the courier's stamps.
8. {{If this plan family coexists with an INCUMBENT protocol (the project already
   keeps its own live TASKS/EXECUTION docs): state explicitly which docs THIS family
   owns and which belong to the incumbent — never write to the incumbent's docs from
   these prompts; report the pending incumbent update to {{owner}} instead.}}
9. **COMMIT AND PUSH the docs repo — the doc-sync does not exist until pushed.**
   `{{docs_dir}}` is its own NESTED git repo inside the container; the harness /
   environment header reports the working directory as "not a git repo" — that is the
   CONTAINER, don't believe it: verify with `git -C {{docs_dir}} status`. The doc-sync executor closes with
   `git -C {{docs_dir}} add <explicit paths>` (the docs, `map/`, `handoffs/<Wave>-*`, qa files; never `add -A`: other
   sessions share the tree) `&& git commit -F <msg> && git push`. An
   unpushed logbook is invisible to the next session and to {{owner}} from another
   machine (lesson learned: a whole wave's docs once sat uncommitted for days).

The links block appears exactly once per delivery close — in the orchestrator's final message, after the last courier RETURN. Progress turns, 'Para ti' notes, answers and audits carry no links and no `=== LINKS ===` marker; with several couriers (batches, `SendMessage` replies) only the last RETURN's block is pasted. No agent other than the courier returns URLs.

Merge nuance (every wave, ⚠gate or not): phase d leaves the wave ⛔ "verified, pending merge by the Reviewer", never ✅.
Phase e flips it ✅ after the human merge (and the deploy, when the wave ships) — every wave has its own e, so a wave's
done state never waits for the next wave (the digest would show that wave as not ready).

## 3. Delivery / merge policy

| Wave type | Delivery |
|---|---|
| NO-gate | MR opened, pipeline green, simplify pass done, assigned to the Reviewer — never merged by Claude |
| ⚠gate ({{gate_wave_ids}}) | MR opened, pipeline green, simplify pass done, assigned to the Reviewer — never merged by Claude — plus the adversarial verification before the review |

**Claude never merges.** A wave ends with its MR open, pipeline green, simplify pass done and assigned to the Reviewer (`> **Reviewer:**` above); the merge is a DoD-human item and wave N+1 starts only after MR N is merged (merge policy: wait). This applies to the waves' code MRs — the docs repo (ai-brain, any of its three positions) and the marketplaces commit and push to main directly.

Nothing is delivered — MR opened or committed for review — on a diff the
simplify pass has not seen, or on a DoD that predates it (§1.11).

## 4. Phase sessions, handoffs, checkpoint / resume

**One clean session per phase.** A wave runs as phases **a** implement + DoD (ends with the MR open, pipeline green,
simplify pass done) · **b** gate (auditors; their RETURN saved verbatim to `qa/gate-<wave>.md`, wave id in lowercase;
later rounds `qa/gate-<wave>-r<n>.md`, post-prod `qa/gate-<wave>-postprod.md`) · **c** gate fixes (one round
per session, then back to b) · **d** doc-sync + courier close (MR assigned to the Reviewer; the merge stays DoD-human) ·
**e** production close (after the human merge / deploy: smoke, post-prod markers, doc-sync phase B, courier) — or, on a
wave with no deploy, the mini merge close (verify the merge, doc-sync flips the wave ✅, A and B in one message when no
marker is pending, courier only if a registered page changed). EVERY wave has e: d always ends with `Next phase: e`, e with
`none`. A gate PASS skips c; a NO-gate plain wave runs a → d → e; an advisory gate goes c → d with no re-gate. Each phase is one
self-contained §7 prompt and a phase never continues into the next one in the same session.

**Phase start.** Every phase prompt opens with the 3-line MAIN-THREAD GUARD note: hooks enforce 25 Bash / 8 Read calls per
session, no heavy reads (whole docs, long ranges, images), edits ≤ 20 lines, a handoff gate at the 2nd compaction and a lock
at the 3rd — so the main session delegates from its first call. Phase a adds step 0, the `plan-audit --check` seal gate:
`plan-audit --brain {{docs_dir}} --check --wave <W>` must exit 0 before any code (exit 2 = this wave's plan changed since the
seal; exit 1 = missing, FAIL or unsealed). Remedy inline: the scoped `{{docs_dir}}/scripts/plan-audit-brief.md` audit (the
full Step 6b audit when there is no report), `qa/plan-audit.md` saved verbatim, the owner's decisions applied by an
executor, `plan-audit --seal`, then phase a continues; owner away → handoff `Result: fail` · `Next phase: a`. After every
doc-sync, phases d and e re-check the whole plan (§2b) and re-audit stale later waves before closing. The seal covers every heading
block naming the wave, these §7 phase prompts included, so planning runs plan-audit AFTER the §7 prompts are written and
BEFORE they are rendered or handed to {{owner}} (skill Step 6b).

**Handoff file.** `{{docs_dir}}/handoffs/<Wave>-<nn>-<phase>.md` (nn = 01, 02 … within the wave), written by the main
session from `{{docs_dir}}/scripts/handoff.md.tmpl`. Header bullets, labels exact: `- **Wave:**` · `- **Phase closed:**` ·
`- **Result:**` (done | partial | fail) · `- **Next phase:**` (a | b | c | d | e | none) · `- **Branch/MR:**` · `- **State:**`
· `- **Open:**` · `- **Agents used:**` · `- **Main thread:**` (stamped by the hook; the template leaves it empty) ·
`- **Next prompt:**` (first line of the next phase prompt); then the logbook notes the doc-sync sheet needs. The next phase
reads ONLY the newest handoff (status-digest names it on its PHASE line); doc-sync B commits the wave's handoffs.

**Phase close.** The last lines of every phase session are the exact token `HANDOFF: <path>` and one sentence telling
{{owner}} to run /clear and paste the §7 prompt `<Wave> · <next>` (or to run `status-digest --brain {{docs_dir}} --prompt`).
In phases d and e the links block of the last courier RETURN still closes the message, right after those two lines (§2b
step 5: the `artifact-guard` Stop hook rejects text after the last link).

**Checkpoint / resume.** Same-phase rule, for every phase: a phase that cannot finish hands off to itself. If a session
dies or is gated mid-phase (STOP IF, the 2nd compaction, a plan-audit decision the owner has not made yet), the main
session writes the handoff anyway with `Result: partial | fail` and `Next phase:` = the same phase, never `none`. Partial state lives in (a) the wave branch's
checkpoint commits and (b) that handoff: internal tasks closed, exact step in progress, which executor tier held each open
task and on which rung of the ladder (attempt log), unmerged worktrees, whether the simplify pass already ran and over
which checkpoint commit (so the resuming session neither repeats nor skips it). The resuming session runs
`status-digest --brain {{docs_dir}}` (LAST LOG gives the resume point — the newest logbook entry's next/blocked/agents —,
its PHASE line names the newest handoff and `--prompt` prints the exact prompt to paste), reads only that handoff, checks out
the branch and continues from that rung — it does NOT restart, and it does NOT re-brief tasks whose worktree is intact.
Legacy brains (one prompt per wave) keep the `[PARTIAL]` logbook entry with its `**Next:**` line (`--entry K` prints it).

